+## 2026-10-09 — Stage 2 (the window) is needed after all: past the window, stage 1 reads freed KV
+
+**VERDICT:** `SHIPPED` · **GATE:** `test_non_causal_sliding_window_never_reads_freed_blocks`
+(fails on stage 1 with "read a freed block outside the window") plus the DFlash2 long-context
+serving rows in `DEVLOG-dflash2.md` 2026-10-09 (4.6k / 14.6k / 24.4k prompts: 0 % accepted on
+stage 1 → 3.66 / 3.70 / 3.85 tokens/step).
+
+The 2026-09-17 entry judged the superset mask harmless because Muse ran at 8k with short
+prompts. The hazard is not the extra keys, though. A sliding layer's KV blocks are **freed** once
+they fall out of its window, so a non-causal sliding batch that attends `[0, seq_len)` reads
+freed or placeholder blocks as soon as the context exceeds the window. For the DFlash2 drafter
+(sliding 2048) that is exactly 0 % acceptance from ~2–4k context on.
+
+The fix needs no kernel change. For a non-causal batch with `window > 0`, `forward_paged` keeps
+the window for the gather clip (`_gather_clip_start`, first row's window start) and passes
+`q_abs_offset = seq_len`, `window = nc_window + n_q` to the existing mask. Every key now sits
+before the row, so the causal test never fires. The window test then masks keys `nc_window` or
+more behind each row's true position. That is the reference's symmetric window, whose right
+side a block of n_q rows never reaches. The direct-paged branch gets the same arguments. The
+clip and the k-loop start are the ones causal sliding layers already use, so the
+`GATHER_CLIP_MARGIN` contract carries over unchanged.
+
